  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 132 AM CDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\nDuring the end of the week and into the weekend, the upper-level \nridge attempts to shift westward towards our region again. \nHowever, shortwave troughs moving across central portions of the \nCONUS will dampen the ridge. So, while temperatures remain above \nnormal through the weekend (mainly in the mid-to-upper 90s), they \ngradually cool such that by Sunday highs in the low-to-mid 90s \nbecome commonplace. By the end of the weekend, guidance still \ndepicts a front moving into our area, which will usher in highs \nthat are at least closer to seasonal norms for late September \n(upper 80s and mid 90s). Meanwhile, west Texas and southeast New \nMexico will be under southwesterly flow aloft thanks to our \nrelative proximity to the aforementioned troughs moving north of \nthe area. Disturbances in the upper-level flow, coupled with \nplentiful moisture (PWATs between 1.0-1.5 inches) will encourage \nmore widespread rain chances beginning Saturday (Friday the best \nrain chances will still mainly be for westernmost portions of the \narea). Right now, rain chances range between 20-50% for a large \nportion of the region Saturday and Sunday. Rain chances may also \nlinger into next week as well, but confidence is a bit lower that \nfar out. In any case, it still looks like a larger portion of the \narea will see some beneficial rain chances soon, along with more \nseasonable temperatures.\n\nSprang",
